{"type":"standard","title":"Involution (mathematics)","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Involution (mathematics)</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q846862","titles":{"canonical":"Involution_(mathematics)","normalized":"Involution (mathematics)","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Involution (mathematics)</span></span>"},"pageid":293450,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Involution.svg/330px-Involution.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":330,"height":168},"originalimage":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Involution.svg/960px-Involution.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":788,"height":400},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1372904372","tid":"bb1706d0-a71b-11f1-9233-ddd087ffb35e","timestamp":"2026-09-02T22:14:57Z","description":"Function that is its own inverse","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Involution_(mathematics)","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Involution_(mathematics)?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Involution_(mathematics)?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Involution_(mathematics)"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Involution_(mathematics)","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Involution_(mathematics)","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Involution_(mathematics)?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Involution_(mathematics)"}},"extract":"In mathematics, an involution, involutory function, or self-inverse function is a function f that is its own inverse, f(f(x)) = x\nfor all x in the domain of f. Equivalently, applying f twice produces the original value.","extract_html":"<p>In mathematics, an <b>involution</b>, <b>involutory function</b>, or <b>self-inverse function</b> is a function <span class=\"texhtml mvar\" style=\"font-style:italic\">f</span> that is its own inverse, <span class=\"texhtml \"><i>f</i>(<i>f</i>(<i>x</i>)) = <i>x</i></span>\nfor all <span class=\"texhtml mvar\" style=\"font-style:italic\">x</span> in the domain of <span class=\"texhtml \"><i>f</i></span>. Equivalently, applying <span class=\"texhtml mvar\" style=\"font-style:italic\">f</span> twice produces the original value.</p>"}